Fix the following CTL2 DENORMALIZER transform so that it compiles and does what the specification says. List every problem you fix with the reason, then give the complete corrected code.

Specification:
1. Input ParcelIn has consignment_id (string, non-null), parcel_id (string, non-null), weight_kg (decimal, nullable), and cancelled (boolean, non-null). Rows are grouped by consignment_id, and each group has at least one row. Output ManifestOut has consignment_id (string), parcel_count (integer), parcel_ids (string), total_weight_kg (decimal), and has_heavy (boolean).
2. Retain the consignment_id for each group. Cancelled parcels contribute nothing to the counts, IDs, weight, or heavy flag.
3. parcel_count counts non-cancelled parcels. parcel_ids joins their IDs in input order with a comma only between IDs; the empty string represents a group with no active parcels.
4. A null weight contributes 0D to total_weight_kg. has_heavy is true only when some non-cancelled parcel has a non-null weight strictly greater than 20D.
5. Emit one manifest even for an all-cancelled group, then reset every group value before the next group.

Current transform:
```ctl
//#CTL2:COMPILE
string consignmentKey = null;
integer parcelCount = 0;
string parcelIds = "";
decimal totalWeight = 0D;
boolean heavy = false;
function integer append() {
    if (isnull(consignmentKey)) consignmentKey = $in.0.consignment_id;
    parcelCount = parcelCount + 1;
    parcelIds = parcelIds + $in.0.parcel_id + ",";
    totalWeight = totalWeight + $in.0.weight_kg;
    if (!isnull($in.0.weight_kg) && $in.0.weight_kg >= 20D) heavy = true;
    return OK;
}
function integer transform() {
    $out.0.consignment_id = consignmentKey;
    $out.0.parcel_count = parcelCount;
    $out.0.parcel_ids = parcelIds;
    $out.0.total_weight_kg = totalWeight;
    $out.0.has_heavy = heavy;
    return OK;
}
function void clean() {
    consignmentKey = null;
    parcelCount = 0;
    totalWeight = 0D;
    heavy = false;
}
```

Fix the following CTL2 NORMALIZER transform so that it compiles and does what the specification says. List every problem you fix with the reason, then give the complete corrected code.

Input port 0 has meter_id: string, reading_day: date, peak_kwh: integer (nullable), offpeak_kwh: integer (nullable), and solar_kwh: integer (nullable). meter_id and reading_day are non-null. Output port 0 has meter_id: string, reading_day: date, tariff: string, kwh: integer, and ordinal: integer.

Specification:
1. Expand each input row into one output row for each positive, non-null kWh field, ordered PEAK, OFFPEAK, then SOLAR. Emit zero rows if none qualify.
2. A zero or negative value in any kWh field is omitted; null is also omitted without a runtime failure.
3. tariff is the matching uppercase field label and kwh is its original positive integer value.
4. ordinal is the zero-based index among rows actually emitted for that meter record.
5. Copy meter_id and reading_day unchanged to each emitted row.
6. Build the per-input selection afresh so no tariff from a previous meter is emitted.

Current transform:
```ctl
//#CTL2
string[] tariffs;
integer[] amounts;
function integer count() {
    tariffs = [];
    amounts = [];
    if (!isnull($in.0.peak_kwh) && $in.0.peak_kwh >= 0) {
        append(tariffs, "PEAK"); append(amounts, $in.0.peak_kwh);
    }
    if (!isnull($in.0.offpeak_kwh)) {
        append(tariffs, "OFFPEAK"); append(amounts, $in.0.offpeak_kwh);
    }
    if ($in.0.solar_kwh > 0) {
        append(tariffs, "SOLAR"); append(amounts, $in.0.solar_kwh);
    }
    return length(tariffs) - 1;
}
function integer transform(integer idx) {
    $out.0.meter_id = $in.0.meter_id;
    $out.0.reading_day = dateAdd($in.0.reading_day, 1L, day);
    $out.0.tariff = tariffs[idx];
    $out.0.kwh = amounts[idx];
    $out.0.ordinal = idx + 1;
    return OK;
}
```

